1132: and theorem~\ref{thm:reach} would fall with it. This section derives the input.
1133: 
1134: \begin{theorem}[Vanishing of the quadratic trace]
1135: \label{thm:gten}
1136: For a self-dual five-form in ten dimensions, the free stress tensor has vanishing
1137: trace. In an even-degree interaction expansion, the trace can first
1138: contribute at degree four; nonvanishing at that degree is a separate input.
1139: \end{theorem}
1140: 
1141: \begin{proof}
1142: Write the free stress tensor of a $p$-form field strength in $d$ dimensions in
1143: its general improved form,
1144: \begin{equation}
1145: \begin{gathered}
1146: T_{\mu\nu}
1147:  \;=\; \frac{1}{(p-1)!}\,F_{\mu\alpha_2\dots\alpha_p}\,
1148:         F_\nu{}^{\alpha_2\dots\alpha_p}
1149:  \;-\; c\,\eta_{\mu\nu}\,\langle F,F\rangle,
1150: \\[2pt]
1151: \langle F,G\rangle \;=\; F_{\mu_1\dots\mu_p}G^{\mu_1\dots\mu_p},
1152: \end{gathered}
1153: \label{eq:stresstensor}
1154: \end{equation}
1155: with $c$ an improvement coefficient left free. Contracting with $\eta^{\mu\nu}$,
1156: the first term returns $\langle F,F\rangle$ up to the normalisation carried by
1157: the factorial and the second returns $-\,c\,d\,\langle F,F\rangle$, so
1158: \begin{equation}
1159: T^{\mu}{}_{\mu} \;=\; (1 - c\,d)\,\langle F,F\rangle .
1160: \label{eq:tracescalar}
1161: \end{equation}
1162: Whatever $c$ may be, the trace is a multiple of the single scalar
1163: $\langle F,F\rangle$; no other quadratic Lorentz invariant exists, since the only
1164: way to contract two copies of a $p$-form into a scalar is the full contraction.
1165: The conclusion below is therefore independent of the improvement term and of the
1166: overall normalisation.
1167: 
1168: It remains to show that this one scalar vanishes. Specialise to $p = 5$, $d = 10$,
1169: so that $F$ is a middle-degree form of \emph{odd} degree in an even-dimensional
1170: space, and consider the top form $F\wedge F$. Interchanging two factors of a
1171: $q$-form costs $(-1)^{q^2} = (-1)^{q}$, which for $q = 5$ is $-1$; hence
1172: \begin{equation}
1173: F\wedge F \;=\; -\,F\wedge F
1174: \qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad
1175: F\wedge F \;\equiv\; 0
1176: \label{eq:wedgevanishes}
1177: \end{equation}
1178: identically, for every five-form whatsoever. Since
1179: $\langle F,\star F\rangle\,\mathrm{vol} \propto F\wedge F$, the pairing
1180: $\langle F,\star F\rangle$ vanishes as well. On either eigenspace of the Hodge
1181: star we have $\star F = \pm F$, and therefore
1182: \begin{equation}
1183: \langle F,F\rangle
1184:  \;=\; \pm\,\langle F,\star F\rangle
1185:  \;=\; 0 .
1186: \label{eq:normvanishes}
1187: \end{equation}
1188: Substituting \eqref{eq:normvanishes} into \eqref{eq:tracescalar} gives
1189: $T^{\mu}{}_{\mu} = 0$ for every $c$.
1190: \end{proof}
1191: 
1192: Two features of this argument are worth isolating, because both are places where
1193: a plausible-looking variant of the calculation gives a different answer.
1194: 
1195: The vanishing in \eqref{eq:wedgevanishes} is a statement about form parity alone
1196: and holds for \emph{any} five-form, self-dual or not; self-duality enters only at
1197: \eqref{eq:normvanishes}, to convert $\langle F,\star F\rangle$ into
1198: $\langle F,F\rangle$. The two steps are independent, and a test suite that
1199: checked only self-dual samples would confirm the conclusion without ever
1200: exercising the mechanism behind it. Ours checks both eigenspaces --- the argument

